1
00:00:03,978 --> 00:00:07,568
Have you ever wondered what your body does while you play?

2
00:00:07,588 --> 00:00:11,688
Meet Maya. She's eleven, and a ball is flying her way.

3
00:00:11,933 --> 00:00:15,041
Many body parts will work together to catch it.

4
00:00:15,061 --> 00:00:17,586
Let's slow it down and look inside.

5
00:00:19,397 --> 00:00:23,578
It starts with light bouncing off the ball and into Maya's eyes.

6
00:00:23,598 --> 00:00:28,035
At the back of the eye, a thin layer called the retina reacts to light.

7
00:00:28,055 --> 00:00:33,834
It turns light into electrical signals that travel along the optic nerve to the brain.

8
00:00:33,854 --> 00:00:40,376
A nerve is a bundle of long, thin fibers from cells called neurons, which carry messages.

9
00:00:40,396 --> 00:00:45,377
Her brain processes the signals, so Maya can judge where the ball is going.

10
00:00:45,397 --> 00:00:51,147
Then her brain sends signals down the spinal cord and along nerves to her muscles.

11
00:00:52,959 --> 00:00:55,728
Muscles can only pull, never push.

12
00:00:55,748 --> 00:01:00,154
When a signal reaches a muscle, it contracts, meaning it gets shorter.

13
00:01:00,174 --> 00:01:03,542
Tough cords called tendons attach muscles to bones.

14
00:01:03,562 --> 00:01:06,956
A shortening muscle pulls a bone, moving a joint.

15
00:01:06,976 --> 00:01:12,052
Muscles on the front of her thigh straighten her knee. Muscles on the back bend it.

16
00:01:12,072 --> 00:01:14,574
They take turns, and Maya runs.

17
00:01:14,594 --> 00:01:19,021
Arm muscles lift her hands, and her fingers close around the ball.

18
00:01:19,041 --> 00:01:20,416
Got it!

19
00:01:22,920 --> 00:01:27,773
To release energy from food, her working muscle cells need oxygen.

20
00:01:27,793 --> 00:01:30,257
So Maya breathes faster and deeper.

21
00:01:30,277 --> 00:01:35,297
Air flows through her nose and mouth, down her windpipe, and into her lungs.

22
00:01:35,317 --> 00:01:39,135
The lungs hold millions of tiny air sacs called alveoli.

23
00:01:39,155 --> 00:01:43,566
Their walls are so thin that oxygen passes through into the blood.

24
00:01:43,586 --> 00:01:50,786
Meanwhile, carbon dioxide, a waste gas from working cells, moves the other way and is breathed out.

25
00:01:52,560 --> 00:01:57,954
The heart is a fist-sized muscle that pumps blood through tubes called blood vessels.

26
00:01:57,974 --> 00:02:01,906
The right side pumps blood to the lungs to pick up oxygen.

27
00:02:01,926 --> 00:02:07,529
The left side pumps that oxygen-rich blood to her body, including her leg muscles.

28
00:02:07,549 --> 00:02:12,104
Blood also delivers nutrients and carries carbon dioxide away.

29
00:02:12,124 --> 00:02:17,449
While Maya runs, her heart beats faster, sending more blood to her muscles.

30
00:02:19,245 --> 00:02:21,967
Those nutrients come from Maya's lunch.

31
00:02:21,987 --> 00:02:26,204
Digestion breaks food into pieces small enough for the body to use.

32
00:02:26,224 --> 00:02:28,189
Her teeth crush the food.

33
00:02:28,209 --> 00:02:34,818
Her stomach squeezes and mixes it with acid and enzymes, substances that help break food apart.

34
00:02:34,838 --> 00:02:39,813
In the small intestine, nutrients such as sugars pass into the blood.

35
00:02:41,616 --> 00:02:46,783
Blood also flows through the kidneys, two bean-shaped organs in her lower back.

36
00:02:46,803 --> 00:02:52,498
They filter the blood, removing waste and extra water, which leave the body as urine.

37
00:02:52,518 --> 00:02:55,839
They also help balance water and salts in her blood.

38
00:02:55,859 --> 00:03:02,141
If Maya sweats a lot, her kidneys return more water to the blood, so she makes less urine.

39
00:03:03,942 --> 00:03:06,183
Now Maya sits down to rest.

40
00:03:06,203 --> 00:03:11,521
Her muscles need less oxygen now, so her breathing and heart rate gradually slow.

41
00:03:11,541 --> 00:03:13,232
It takes a few minutes.

42
00:03:13,252 --> 00:03:19,371
She keeps breathing a little faster to clear extra carbon dioxide and catch up on oxygen.

43
00:03:19,391 --> 00:03:22,041
Then she's back to her resting pace.

44
00:03:23,934 --> 00:03:26,259
So, one quick catch took teamwork.

45
00:03:26,279 --> 00:03:31,013
Her eyes and brain helped her spot the ball, and nerves carried signals.

46
00:03:31,033 --> 00:03:33,036
Muscles pulled on bones.

47
00:03:33,056 --> 00:03:36,896
Her lungs took in oxygen, and her heart pumped it around.

48
00:03:36,916 --> 00:03:42,152
Digestion supplied nutrients, and her kidneys helped keep her blood balanced.

49
00:03:42,516 --> 00:03:48,421
Something to think about: when you're asleep, what happens to your breathing and heartbeat, and why?
